sqrt(5) = 2.236067977499790
phi = (1+sqrt(5))/2 = 1.618033988749895
phi^2 = phi + 1 = 2.618033988749895
1/phi = phi - 1 = 0.618033988749895
5/phi = 3.090169943749474 = 3.090169943749475
sqrt(5)*phi = 3.618033988749895 = phi^2 + phi - 1? = 3.236067977499790

Q-transform of 1/sqrt(5):
  1/sqrt(5) = 0.447213595499958
  Q(1/sqrt(5)) = (1 + 1/sqrt(5)) / (1 - 1/sqrt(5))
            = 2.618033988749894
  phi^2 = 2.618033988749895
  Q(1/sqrt(5)) = phi^2? True

Proof: Q(1/sqrt(5)) = (sqrt(5)+1)/(sqrt(5)-1) = (sqrt(5)+1)^2/4 = (6+2*sqrt(5))/4 = (3+sqrt(5))/2 = phi^2
  (3+sqrt(5))/2 = 2.618033988749895
  phi^2 = 2.618033988749895
  Match: True

Q-transform of Fibonacci ratios F_n/F_{n+1}:
  Q(F_1/F_2) = Q(1/1) = inf,  F_3/F_0 = 2/0 = inf  Match: False
  Q(F_2/F_3) = Q(1/2) = 3.00000000,  F_4/F_1 = 3/1 = 3.00000000  Match: True
  Q(F_3/F_4) = Q(2/3) = 5.00000000,  F_5/F_2 = 5/1 = 5.00000000  Match: True
  Q(F_4/F_5) = Q(3/5) = 4.00000000,  F_6/F_3 = 8/2 = 4.00000000  Match: True
  Q(F_5/F_6) = Q(5/8) = 4.33333333,  F_7/F_4 = 13/3 = 4.33333333  Match: True
  Q(F_6/F_7) = Q(8/13) = 4.20000000,  F_8/F_5 = 21/5 = 4.20000000  Match: True
  Q(F_7/F_8) = Q(13/21) = 4.25000000,  F_9/F_6 = 34/8 = 4.25000000  Match: True
  Q(F_8/F_9) = Q(21/34) = 4.23076923,  F_10/F_7 = 55/13 = 4.23076923  Match: True
  Q(F_9/F_10) = Q(34/55) = 4.23809524,  F_11/F_8 = 89/21 = 4.23809524  Match: True
  Q(F_10/F_11) = Q(55/89) = 4.23529412,  F_12/F_9 = 144/34 = 4.23529412  Match: True
  Q(F_11/F_12) = Q(89/144) = 4.23636364,  F_13/F_10 = 233/55 = 4.23636364  Match: True
  Q(F_12/F_13) = Q(144/233) = 4.23595506,  F_14/F_11 = 377/89 = 4.23595506  Match: True
  Q(F_13/F_14) = Q(233/377) = 4.23611111,  F_15/F_12 = 610/144 = 4.23611111  Match: True
  Q(F_14/F_15) = Q(377/610) = 4.23605150,  F_16/F_13 = 987/233 = 4.23605150  Match: True

The Cayley skip-3 property: Q(F_n/F_{n+1}) = F_{n+2}/F_{n-1}
Algebraic proof: Q(a/b) = (b+a)/(b-a). With a=F_n, b=F_{n+1}:
  b+a = F_{n+1}+F_n = F_{n+2}
  b-a = F_{n+1}-F_n = F_{n-1}
  So Q(F_n/F_{n+1}) = F_{n+2}/F_{n-1}. QED (skip-3: from (n,n+1) to (n-1,n+2))

========================================================================
  7. PISANO PERIODS pi(m) FOR FUNDAMENTAL PRIMES
========================================================================

The Pisano period pi(m) is the period of F_n mod m.

  pi(  2) =    3   (2 -> period 3 = 3)
  pi(  3) =    8   (3 -> period 8 = 2^3)
  pi(  4) =    6   (2^2 -> period 6 = 2 * 3)
  pi(  5) =   20   (5 -> period 20 = 2^2 * 5)
  pi(  6) =   24   (2 * 3 -> period 24 = 2^3 * 3)
  pi(  7) =   16   (7 -> period 16 = 2^4)
  pi(  8) =   12   (2^3 -> period 12 = 2^2 * 3)
  pi(  9) =   24   (3^2 -> period 24 = 2^3 * 3)
  pi( 10) =   60   (2 * 5 -> period 60 = 2^2 * 3 * 5)
  pi( 11) =   10   (11 -> period 10 = 2 * 5)
  pi( 12) =   24   (2^2 * 3 -> period 24 = 2^3 * 3)
  pi( 13) =   28   (13 -> period 28 = 2^2 * 7)
  pi( 21) =   16   (3 * 7 -> period 16 = 2^4)
  pi( 42) =   48   (2 * 3 * 7 -> period 48 = 2^4 * 3)
  pi( 47) =   32   (47 -> period 32 = 2^5)
  pi( 77) =   80   (7 * 11 -> period 80 = 2^4 * 5)
  pi( 55) =   20   (5 * 11 -> period 20 = 2^2 * 5)

KEY VALUES for fundamental primes:
  pi( 2) =   3
    F_n mod 2: [0, 1, 1] | [0, 1, 1]...
  pi( 3) =   8
    F_n mod 3: [0, 1, 1, 2, 0, 2, 2, 1] | [0, 1, 1]...
  pi( 5) =  20
    F_n mod 5: [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 0, 3, 3, 1, 4, 0, 4, 4, 3, 2, 0, 2, 2, 4, 1] | [0, 1, 1]...
  pi( 7) =  16
    F_n mod 7: [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 1, 6, 0, 6, 6, 5, 4, 2, 6, 1] | [0, 1, 1]...
  pi(11) =  10
    F_n mod 11: [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 2, 10, 1] | [0, 1, 1]...
  pi(13) =  28
    F_n mod 13: [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 0, 8, 8, 3, 11, 1, 12, 0, 12, 12, 11, 10, 8, 5, 0, 5, 5, 10, 2, 12, 1] | [0, 1, 1]...

Pisano period relationships:
  pi(2) = 3
  pi(3) = 8
  pi(5) = 20
  pi(7) = 16
  pi(11) = 10
  pi(2)*pi(3) = 24, pi(6) = 24
  lcm(pi(2),pi(3)) = 24, pi(6) = 24
  pi(21) = 16, lcm(pi(3),pi(7)) = 16

Known formula: pi(p) | p-1 if p = +-1 mod 5, pi(p) | 2(p+1) if p = +-2 mod 5
  p= 2 = 2 mod 5: pi(2)=3, 2(p+1)=6, divides? 2(p+1)
  p= 3 = 3 mod 5: pi(3)=8, 2(p+1)=8, divides? 2(p+1)
  p= 5 = 0 mod 5: pi(5)=20 (special)
  p= 7 = 2 mod 5: pi(7)=16, 2(p+1)=16, divides? 2(p+1)
  p=11 = 1 mod 5: pi(11)=10, (p-1)=10, divides? p-1
  p=13 = 3 mod 5: pi(13)=28, 2(p+1)=28, divides? 2(p+1)
  p=17 = 2 mod 5: pi(17)=36, 2(p+1)=36, divides? 2(p+1)
  p=19 = 4 mod 5: pi(19)=18, (p-1)=18, divides? p-1
  p=23 = 3 mod 5: pi(23)=48, 2(p+1)=48, divides? 2(p+1)
  p=29 = 4 mod 5: pi(29)=14, (p-1)=28, divides? p-1
  p=31 = 1 mod 5: pi(31)=30, (p-1)=30, divides? p-1
  p=37 = 2 mod 5: pi(37)=76, 2(p+1)=76, divides? 2(p+1)
  p=41 = 1 mod 5: pi(41)=40, (p-1)=40, divides? p-1
  p=43 = 3 mod 5: pi(43)=88, 2(p+1)=88, divides? 2(p+1)
  p=47 = 2 mod 5: pi(47)=32, 2(p+1)=96, divides? 2(p+1)

